<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

	<!--
		Dragon orbs (2026-09-22). Furniture that is never built, only bought: the
		dragonian caravan trader sells them, and no other trader can, because both
		defs are excluded by name from the three vanilla stock generators that roll
		BuildingsFurniture (Patches/ExcludeDragonContentFromVanillaStock.xml).

		Why there is no designationCategory and no costList. That pair is exactly
		what puts a def in the Architect menu, so leaving both off is what makes an
		orb unsellable-but-unbuildable: it exists only as something a trader hands
		over. FurnitureBase would be the natural parent, but it hard-codes
		<designationCategory>Furniture</designationCategory> - so the fields it would
		have given us are repeated here instead, and the category is left out.
		<minifiedDef> is what makes the orb an object at all: it is what a trader's
		stock generator wraps the building in (the same machinery vanilla's own
		furniture trade uses, which is why a bought dresser arrives as a minified
		dresser), and it is what lets the player install, uninstall and carry her orb
		like any other piece of furniture.

		Two variants, one per kind of orb the wiki describes:

		  PMM_DragonOrb       the calling orb, a clear stone. Only at night, and only
		                      while it is installed on a map, it draws a wild dragon
		                      to that map whatever the habitat (Source/Reptiles/
		                      ReptileWandersIn.cs). It spends itself doing it: once a
		                      dragon has answered, the calling orb quietly becomes
		                      the glowing orb below.
		  PMM_DragonOrbDecor  the ever-flow orb: coloured, prettier, and nothing more
		                      than a porch light and a ward. It is what a spent
		                      calling orb becomes, and it can also be bought alone.

		Beauty is fixed rather than stuffable, and deliberately so. The player can
		never choose a material for a def she cannot build, and nothing in vanilla
		picks a stuff for a minified building a trader stocks - a stuffable orb would
		be spawned without one and would lose its beauty, its hit points and its art.

		Art: Textures/Things/Building/PMM_DragonOrb.png (64x64), shared by both
		variants. They are told apart by their light, not their stone: the calling
		orb is the dimmer, paler one - moonlight with a slight drift of violet, where
		the glowing orb is a full violet - and that light is what the player sees
		change on the night the orb pays for itself.
	-->
	<ThingDef Name="PMM_DragonOrbBase" ParentName="BuildingBase" Abstract="True">
		<thingClass>Building</thingClass>
		<size>(1,1)</size>
		<rotatable>false</rotatable>
		<altitudeLayer>Building</altitudeLayer>
		<passability>PassThroughOnly</passability>
		<pathCost>14</pathCost>
		<fillPercent>0.15</fillPercent>
		<graphicData>
			<texPath>Things/Building/PMM_DragonOrb</texPath>
			<graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
		</graphicData>
		<thingCategories>
			<li>BuildingsFurniture</li>
		</thingCategories>
		<minifiedDef>MinifiedThing</minifiedDef>
		<building>
			<uninstallWork>1200</uninstallWork>
		</building>
		<statBases>
			<MaxHitPoints>150</MaxHitPoints>
			<Mass>12</Mass>
			<WorkToBuild>2500</WorkToBuild>
			<Flammability>0</Flammability>
		</statBases>
		<placeWorkers>
			<li>PlaceWorker_GlowRadius</li>
		</placeWorkers>
	</ThingDef>

	<!-- The calling orb: the one a dragon answers. -->
	<ThingDef ParentName="PMM_DragonOrbBase">
		<defName>PMM_DragonOrb</defName>
		<label>dragon orb</label>
		<description>A fist-sized gem set in a dragon-carved stone, on a plinth low enough to stand beside a door. The stone is clear, and the light moving inside it is the colour of moonlight with a breath of violet in it.\n\nDragonian households set one by the porch and let it burn all night. A wild dragon can see that light from far out in the dark, and a dragon who comes to look does not leave again: she stays on the map like any wild creature, and she can be tamed. The gem pays for the visit. Once a dragon has answered it, the orb dims to an ordinary glow and calls nothing else.</description>
		<statBases>
			<Beauty>15</Beauty>
			<MarketValue>420</MarketValue>
		</statBases>
		<comps>
			<!-- Each variant declares its own glower: the light is what tells the two
			     orbs apart. (Child <li> list nodes append to the parent's list, so this
			     base could hold shared comps - it holds none, and each variant lists
			     the one comp it wants.) -->
			<li Class="CompProperties_Glower">
				<glowRadius>8</glowRadius>
				<!-- Pale lavender: moonlight with a slight drift of the violet the glowing
				     orb carries, so the two are still told apart by saturation rather than
				     by hue. More red lifts it towards purple, more blue towards moonlight. -->
				<glowColor>(186,178,255,0)</glowColor>
			</li>
		</comps>
	</ThingDef>

	<!-- The ever-flow orb: the pretty one, and what a spent calling orb becomes. -->
	<ThingDef ParentName="PMM_DragonOrbBase">
		<defName>PMM_DragonOrbDecor</defName>
		<label>glowing dragon orb</label>
		<description>A fist-sized gem set in a dragon-carved stone, on a plinth low enough to stand beside a door. This one is coloured, and its light flows through it without ever going out - the spell the dragonians call ever-flow.\n\nIt is a porch light and a ward against the dark, and nothing more: dragons do not answer a coloured orb. It is also what a calling orb becomes, on the night its light finally pays for itself.</description>
		<statBases>
			<Beauty>24</Beauty>
			<MarketValue>150</MarketValue>
		</statBases>
		<comps>
			<li Class="CompProperties_Glower">
				<glowRadius>12</glowRadius>
				<glowColor>(196,150,255,0)</glowColor>
			</li>
		</comps>
	</ThingDef>

</Defs>
